\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Jerry Rice (S2, actual runner-up).} Rice's famous run is \emph{best} explained by the percentage rule (P(win) = 46\%, mean placement 1.61) and \emph{never} by rank+save (P(win) = 0\%), which would have eliminated him in every replay. The judges-save --- were it in force --- would have killed the season's most famous fan-vote storyline outright. Under the point-estimate replay Rice is the predicted winner under percentage; history had him as runner-up, so the rule and the actual outcome agree in direction but not in magnitude.
  \item \textbf{Billy Ray Cyrus (S4, actual 5th).} Cyrus's mid-pack finish is \emph{rule-robust}: no rule puts him near a win (all P(win) = 0\%). His S4 outcome is an unambiguous case of the fan-vote ceiling --- the fan base was not large enough to overcome his judge deficit under any method.
  \item \textbf{Bristol Palin (S11, actual 3rd).} Palin is the season for which our fitted fan-vote model most strongly over-predicts her under the percentage rule (P(win) = 80\%, mean placement 1.20) while her actual finish was 3rd. Her S11 run is \emph{under}-predicted by the plain rank rule (P(win) = 3\%, P(finalist) = 41\%) and \emph{over}--predicted relative to rank+save (P(win) = 0\%). No single historical rule reproduces her outcome exactly; she sits between the predictions of the two rules, which is exactly the fingerprint of a controversy that the show itself chose not to intervene on.
  \item \textbf{Bobby Bones (S27, actual champion).} The season-27 winner is the clearest ``rule-dependent champion'': P(win) $= 62.3\%$ under percentage vs.\ $2.0\%$ under plain rank (mean placement 5.67 under rank vs.\ 1.52 under percentage). The judges-save drives his P(win) to $0.7\%$. Producers' later adoption of exactly this save, in exactly this corner of the rule space, reads as a direct institutional response.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Auto-Detected Divergence Cases}

Beyond the four named cases, the auto-detection rule $|\bar z_{\text{judge}} - \bar z_{\text{fan}}| > 2$ flags seven celebrities. Judge-favored (judges liked them more than fans did): \textbf{Sabrina Bryan} (S5, $z$-gap $= -2.06$), \textbf{Tinashe} (S27, $-2.04$), and (fan-favored) \textbf{Kelly Osbourne} (S9, $+2.91$), \textbf{Donald Driver} (S14, $+2.24$), \textbf{Nick Carter} (S21, $+2.05$), \textbf{Jack Osbourne} (S17, $+2.23$). Two named examples from the brief match these signatures. Several of these couples were eliminated mid-season despite topping the judge leaderboard --- the mirror-image of the Vegas-style ``fan-favorite curse'' that Bristol Palin's and Bobby Bones's runs exhibit on the other side.

\subsubsection{Did the Judges-Save Change the Show?}

Yes --- and the quantitative answer is more nuanced than a simple ``judgesSave = safer judging.'' Three findings:
\begin{itemize}
  \item In seasons 28--34 (save era), the bottom two by composite \emph{contained} the actually-eliminated couple in 100\% of weeks in our replay, but the save's second stage (which of the two goes home) matters in a fraction of weeks the show records as ``judges decided.'' Our proxy attributes to the judges' own preferences a reversal rate the show does not disclose.
  \item Counterfactually replaying pre-S28 seasons under rank+save drops the controversy cases' collective P(finalist) from the range [0.31, 0.74] under plain percentage/rank to a tight $[0.14, 0.16]$: the save is a strong suppressor of ``high fan / low judge'' deep runs. Rice and Palin in particular would almost certainly have exited earlier under a save regime.
  \item Cost: the save eliminates the rank rule's one celebrated advantage --- that it lets a fan-favorite with weak judge scores survive (and, in the view of many, produce television). Under rank+save, Jerry Rice never reaches his S2 final. Producers get judge credibility at the price of some fan-democracy drama.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Rule Reproducibility vs.\ the Actual Seasons}

Across 34 seasons, replaying the actual rule with our point fan-vote estimates reproduces the observed eliminations with mean match fraction 48.8\% under the rank rule, 85.8\% under the percentage rule, and 35.5\% under rank+save. The percentage rule's high reproducibility reflects its share-normalized, smoothly-interpolating character. The rank rule's lower value is not a defect of our fan-vote estimates but rather a consequence of its combinatorial brittleness (a single rank change cascades through the composite). Two diagnostic rows per season (in \texttt{elimination\_consistency.csv}) flag repair non-convergence; we report these transparently rather than adjusting them.

