\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Age:} the only decisively channel-differentiated effect. A one-SD increase in age penalizes the fan channel about \emph{twice} as much as the judge channel (fan-channel $\beta_{\text{age}} = -0.278$ vs.\ judge-channel $\beta_{\text{age}} = -0.125$). Audiences demographically skew young, and the data say age costs popularity-capital roughly double what it costs technical capital.
  \item \textbf{Athlete:} helps both channels (fan $\beta = +0.118$, judge $\beta = +0.068$; or athletes score slightly better with both audiences, with the fan side gaining a bit more --- but the channel contrast CI crosses zero). Athletes on DWTS (NFL players, Olympians, gymnasts) bring both physical learning speed (judges notice) and carry-over fandom (fans notice).
  \item \textbf{Fame tier:} directionally positive in both channels and slightly more so in the fan channel, but not decisively contrasted. Fame helps, and does so roughly equally for judges and fans.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Pro Effects: The Fan Channel's Dominant Driver}

Table \ref{tab:m3-varpart} gives the variance partition --- arguably Model 3's most striking result.

