Across 34 seasons, replaying the actual rule with our point fan-vote estimates reproduces \textbf{89.6\%} of all season-week eliminations (Table \ref{tab:m2-match}), rising to \textbf{92.2\%} on the 14 seasons without double-elimination weeks (11 of which reach match fraction $\ge 0.85$). The percentage rule's high reproducibility reflects its share-normalized, smoothly-interpolating character. The rank rule's lower value is not a defect of our fan-vote estimates but a consequence of its combinatorial brittleness: a single rank change cascades through the integer composite, and one flipped fan rank can strand an, otherwise-predicted, elimination elsewhere in the week. The save era's lower match fraction is consistent with the save's own second stochastic stage (Section \ref{sec:m2-save}); three specific season-weeks (S30 W5, S34 W4, S34 W10) are flagged with non-convergent repair and excluded from the match numerator, contributing to -- but not fully accounting for -- the save era's lower value.

